course: dictionary
lesson: 7861
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. You choose the right meaning by reading the words around the word. Rail is a good example. «The rails steer the wheels, so the driver can only make the train go faster or slower.» Here the rails are the metal bars that trains run on, because the clues are train, wheels and driver.
explain: «Hold the handrail, take one step at a time and put your whole foot on each step.» This time rail means a long bar you hold on to. The clues are steps, holding and grabbing, which all point to a bar beside the stairs. Ask yourself: which meaning fits these words best?
explain: Rail can also mean the railway itself, as in travel by rail. Clothes can hang on a rail, too. Check your choice by putting your meaning into the sentence and reading it again. If it sounds right, you have probably chosen well.
fact: Rail (noun) means a long bar fixed in place, used to hold on to, to hang things on, or to make a fence.
fact: Rails are the metal bars that trains run on.
fact: Rail can also mean the railway system, as in travel by rail.
fact: «Off the rails» means out of control.
fact: Never play on or near railway lines, and cross only at proper crossings when the lights and barriers show it is safe.
exercise: «The rails steer the wheels, so the driver can only make the train go faster or slower.» In this sentence, what are the rails? | A kind of train | A fence round a garden | Long bars for hanging clothes | *Metal bars that trains run on
exercise: «Hold the handrail, take one step at a time.» What is the handrail for? | Playing on at the park | Keeping cows in a field | *Holding on when you go up or down stairs | Hanging up wet towels
exercise: Jayden says rail always means a train track. What is the best answer? | Yes, rail always means a train track | Rail is only a word for a bar on a boat | *Rail has several meanings, so check the words around it | Rail means a type of bird
test: In «a third rail beside the track», what is a rail? | *A metal bar beside the track | A rude remark | A bar that holds towels | A kind of fence round a school
test: Which sentence uses rail to mean the railway system? | «Hold the handrail on the stairs.» | «Keep away from the balcony rails.» | «Dry the towels on the towel rail.» | *«Many families travel by rail on holiday.»
exam: Why must you never play on railway lines? | *Because trains are heavy, cannot swerve, and some rails carry electricity | Because the rails are soft and might break | Because the rails are painted red | Because trains are too slow to stop
